     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Hello everyone,</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Thank you so much Paul for a very useful and well-documented package! </p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I have a question about fitting IRT models with brms. I have put up a reproducible example <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/aammd/1982f0dcd11c739c05598129c9e40b46\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://gist.github.com/aammd/1982f0dcd11c739c05598129c9e40b46&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451459920000&amp;usg=AOvVaw23KPBnOFq4hePJg_WPi7M1\">at this gist</a>, and I’ll also paste it below.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I’m trying to perform this analysis based on two sources: Gelman and Hill’s book, and <a href=\"http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/hierarchical_2pl.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/hierarchical_2pl.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451459920000&amp;usg=AOvVaw01DiyjHKujlpME-2nT1CZN\">this post on the Stan case studies</a>. I’m following the example in the nonlinear modelling vignette.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">each recommend something that I don’t really see how to express in the syntax of <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">bf()</code>. </p>\r\n<ol style=\"margin:1.2em 0px;padding-left:2em\">\r\n<li style=\"margin:0.5em 0px\"><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important;margin:0.5em 0px!important\">Gelman and Hill:  the “three parameter” IRT model is hard to identify, and so one solution they suggest is adding a predictor to one of the terms in the model, and constraining that predictor’s coefficient to have a certain sign. This is supposed to help. However, every time I try to constrain part of a nonlinear equation in brms I get an error from <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">set_prior</code> saying that i can’t use <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">lb = 0</code> when I’m also using the <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">coef</code> argument. (possible solution at the bottom!) </p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li style=\"margin:0.5em 0px\"><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important;margin:0.5em 0px!important\">from Furr’s case study: the “alpha” and “beta” terms of the IRT are two parameters that describe the “items’ being tested. Furr suggests that these be modelled as correlated. However, I don’t see how to indicate that to bf(). Maybe using the new multivariate syntax? Or perhaps the <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">1 | ID1 | group</code> syntax?</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Thank you everyone for your advice!</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Andrew</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:rgb(248,248,255)\"><span>library</span>(brms)\r\n<span>library</span>(tidyverse)\r\n\r\n<span># we&#39;re going to have 5 animals</span>\r\nnspp &lt;- <span>5</span>\r\n<span># in 100 sites</span>\r\nnsites &lt;- <span>100</span>\r\n\r\n<span>#abilities are between -1 and 1, from a normal distribution:</span>\r\nthetas &lt;- rnorm(nspp, <span>0</span>, <span>1.2</span>)\r\n\r\n<span># site difficulty decreases with x</span>\r\nb_x &lt;- - <span>0.7</span>\r\n<span># the average site is an OK place to live, not great but OK</span>\r\nb_0 &lt;- -<span>1</span>\r\n\r\n<span># correlation between slopes and intercepts for sites</span>\r\nsite_cor &lt;- matrix(c(<span>1</span>, -<span>0.3</span>, -<span>0.3</span>, <span>1</span>), nrow = <span>2</span>)\r\n<span># site variance in slopes and inters</span>\r\nsigmas &lt;- c(<span>0.8</span>, <span>0.2</span>)\r\n\r\nsite_cov &lt;- diag(sigmas) %*% site_cor %*% diag(sigmas)\r\n\r\nsite_varying_effects &lt;- MASS::mvrnorm(nsites, mu = c(<span>0</span>, <span>0</span>), Sigma = site_cov)\r\n\r\n<span># site x-values</span>\r\nxs &lt;- runif(nsites, min = -<span>3</span>, max = <span>3</span>)\r\nhist(xs)\r\n\r\n<span># site &quot;difficulty&quot;</span>\r\nbeta_site &lt;- b_0 + xs * b_x + site_varying_effects[,<span>1</span>]\r\n\r\n<span># site discrimination</span>\r\na0 &lt;- <span>0.1</span>\r\n\r\nalpha_site &lt;- exp(a0 + site_varying_effects[,<span>2</span>])\r\n\r\nprojected_response &lt;- expand.grid(site = <span>1</span>:nsites, species = <span>1</span>:nspp) %&gt;% \r\n  tbl_df %&gt;% \r\n  mutate(\r\n    site_x = xs[site],\r\n    site_beta = beta_site[site],\r\n    site_alpha = alpha_site[site],\r\n    spp_theta       = thetas[species],\r\n    response = site_alpha * (spp_theta - site_beta),\r\n    probability = rethinking::inv_logit(response),\r\n    pres_abs = rbinom(length(probability), <span>1</span>, prob = probability))\r\n\r\nprojected_response %&gt;% \r\n  ggplot(aes(x = site_x, y = pres_abs, colour = species, group = species)) + geom_point() +\r\n  stat_smooth(method = <span>&quot;glm&quot;</span>, method.args = list(family = <span>&quot;binomial&quot;</span>))\r\n\r\ninv_logit &lt;- <span>function</span>(x) <span>1</span> / (<span>1</span> + exp(-x))\r\n\r\nirt_3p &lt;- bf(pres_abs ~ inv_logit(alpha * (theta - beta)), \r\n   alpha ~ <span>0</span> + (<span>1</span>|site),\r\n   beta ~ <span>0</span> + intercept + site_x + (<span>1</span>|site),\r\n   theta ~ <span>0</span> + (<span>1</span> | species),\r\n   nl = <span>TRUE</span>)\r\n\r\nget_prior(irt_3p, data = projected_response)\r\n\r\nmy_priors &lt;- c(set_prior(<span>&quot;cauchy(0, 3)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;sd&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;alpha&quot;</span>),\r\n               set_prior(<span>&quot;cauchy(0, 3)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;sd&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;beta&quot;</span>),\r\n               set_prior(<span>&quot;cauchy(0, 3)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;sd&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;theta&quot;</span>),\r\n               set_prior(<span>&quot;normal(0, 2)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;b&quot;</span>, coef = <span>&quot;intercept&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;beta&quot;</span>),\r\n               set_prior(<span>&quot;normal(0, 2)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;b&quot;</span>, coef = <span>&quot;site_x&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;beta&quot;</span>) <span># lb = 0) # produces and error</span>\r\n)\r\n\r\nfit_ir3 &lt;- brm(irt_3p,\r\n               data = projected_response, family = bernoulli(<span>&quot;identity&quot;</span>), \r\n               prior = my_priors)\r\n\r\nsummary(fit_ir3)\r\n</code></pre>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">As I was writing this, I thought of a possible resolution to question #1:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:rgb(248,248,255)\"><span># what if you just write it differently?</span>\r\nirt_3p_v2 &lt;- bf(pres_abs ~ inv_logit(alpha * (theta - (beta + sitex))), \r\n             alpha ~ <span>0</span> + (<span>1</span>|site),\r\n             beta ~ <span>0</span> + intercept + (<span>1</span>|site),\r\n             theta ~ <span>0</span> + (<span>1</span> | species),\r\n             sitex ~ <span>1</span>,\r\n             nl = <span>TRUE</span>)\r\n\r\nget_prior(irt_3p_v2, data = projected_response)\r\n\r\nmy_priors_v2 &lt;- c(set_prior(<span>&quot;cauchy(0, 3)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;sd&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;alpha&quot;</span>),\r\n                  set_prior(<span>&quot;cauchy(0, 3)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;sd&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;beta&quot;</span>),\r\n                  set_prior(<span>&quot;cauchy(0, 3)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;sd&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;theta&quot;</span>),\r\n                  set_prior(<span>&quot;normal(0, 2)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;b&quot;</span>, coef = <span>&quot;intercept&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;beta&quot;</span>),\r\n                  set_prior(<span>&quot;normal(0, 2)&quot;</span>, class = <span>&quot;b&quot;</span>, nlpar = <span>&quot;sitex&quot;</span>, ub = <span>0</span>)\r\n)\r\n\r\nfit_ir3 &lt;- brm(irt_3p_v2,\r\n               data = projected_response, family = bernoulli(<span>&quot;identity&quot;</span>), \r\n               prior = my_priors_v2, chains = <span>1</span>, cores = <span>1</span>, iter = <span>5000</span>)\r\n</code></pre>\r\n<div title=\"MDH:SGVsbG8gZXZlcnlvbmUsPGRpdj48YnI+PC9kaXY+PGRpdj5UaGFuayB5b3Ugc28gbXVjaCBQYXVs\r\nIGZvciBhIHZlcnkgdXNlZnVsIGFuZCB3ZWxsLWRvY3VtZW50ZWQgcGFja2FnZSEmbmJzcDs8L2Rp\r\ndj48ZGl2Pjxicj48L2Rpdj48ZGl2PkkgaGF2ZSBhIHF1ZXN0aW9uIGFib3V0IGZpdHRpbmcgSVJU\r\nIG1vZGVscyB3aXRoIGJybXMuIEkgaGF2ZSBwdXQgdXAgYSByZXByb2R1Y2libGUgZXhhbXBsZSBb\r\nYXQgdGhpcyBnaXN0XShodHRwczovL2dpc3QuZ2l0aHViLmNvbS9hYW1tZC8xOTgyZjBkY2QxMWM3\r\nMzljMDU1OTgxMjljOWU0MGI0NiksIGFuZCBJJ2xsIGFsc28gcGFzdGUgaXQgYmVsb3cuPC9kaXY+\r\nPGRpdj48YnI+PC9kaXY+PGRpdj5JJ20gdHJ5aW5nIHRvIHBlcmZvcm0gdGhpcyBhbmFseXNpcyBi\r\nYXNlZCBvbiB0d28gc291cmNlczogR2VsbWFuIGFuZCBIaWxsJ3MgYm9vaywgYW5kIFt0aGlzIHBv\r\nc3Qgb24gdGhlIFN0YW4gY2FzZSBzdHVkaWVzXShodHRwOi8vbWMtc3Rhbi5vcmcvdXNlcnMvZG9j\r\ndW1lbnRhdGlvbi9jYXNlLXN0dWRpZXMvaGllcmFyY2hpY2FsXzJwbC5odG1sKS4gSSdtIGZvbGxv\r\nd2luZyB0aGUgZXhhbXBsZSBpbiB0aGUgbm9ubGluZWFyIG1vZGVsbGluZyB2aWduZXR0ZS48L2Rp\r\ndj48ZGl2Pjxicj48L2Rpdj48ZGl2PmVhY2ggcmVjb21tZW5kIHNvbWV0aGluZyB0aGF0IEkgZG9u\r\nJ3QgcmVhbGx5IHNlZSBob3cgdG8gZXhwcmVzcyBpbiB0aGUgc3ludGF4IG9mIGBiZigpYC4mbmJz\r\ncDs8L2Rpdj48ZGl2Pjxicj48L2Rpdj48ZGl2PjEuIEdlbG1hbiBhbmQgSGlsbDombmJzcDsgdGhl\r\nICJ0aHJlZSBwYXJhbWV0ZXIiIElSVCBtb2RlbCBpcyBoYXJkIHRvIGlkZW50aWZ5LCBhbmQgc28g\r\nb25lIHNvbHV0aW9uIHRoZXkgc3VnZ2VzdCBpcyBhZGRpbmcgYSBwcmVkaWN0b3IgdG8gb25lIG9m\r\nIHRoZSB0ZXJtcyBpbiB0aGUgbW9kZWwsIGFuZCBjb25zdHJhaW5pbmcgdGhhdCBwcmVkaWN0b3In\r\ncyBjb2VmZmljaWVudCB0byBoYXZlIGEgY2VydGFpbiBzaWduLiBUaGlzIGlzIHN1cHBvc2VkIHRv\r\nIGhlbHAuIEhvd2V2ZXIsIGV2ZXJ5IHRpbWUgSSB0cnkgdG8gY29uc3RyYWluIHBhcnQgb2YgYSBu\r\nb25saW5lYXIgZXF1YXRpb24gaW4gYnJtcyBJIGdldCBhbiBlcnJvciBmcm9tIGBzZXRfcHJpb3Jg\r\nIHNheWluZyB0aGF0IGkgY2FuJ3QgdXNlIGBsYiA9IDBgIHdoZW4gSSdtIGFsc28gdXNpbmcgdGhl\r\nI